  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing deprecated styles**\n\nI\'ve just updated `CheckCircle2` and `Loader2`. Now I\'m focused on identifying any other outdated UI elements or tokens. I noticed the use of standard Tailwind v4 colors, despite the documentation mentioning deprecation of default color names. This needs further investigation.\n\n\n**Analyzing dark mode variables**\n\nI\'m now diving into the dark mode implementation. My previous reliance on the `.dark` class to invert CSS variables, such as `--bg-gray` and `--color-white-var`, appears outdated. Tailwind v4\'s `@variant dark` or built-in dark mode variables look more appropriate.  It seems Tailwind v4 provides built-in CSS variables for all colors (e.g., `--color-gray-900`), so redefining them directly within the theme might be the best approach. Custom variables and mapping them are not needed.\n\n\n**Clarifying dark mode implementation**\n\nI\'m now revisiting the dark mode approach and its compatibility with Tailwind v4. The initial assumption that `.dark` class-based overrides are outdated seems incorrect. Standard CSS variable overriding within the `.dark` class, like `--bg-gray` and `--color-white-var`, appears to be fully compatible with Tailwind v4 and its `@theme` directive. The deprecation I\'d read about seems related to hardcoded hex values; perhaps the suggestion to use `oklch()` or access variables via `var(--color-gray-900)` in favor of hex values is the correct answer. I will keep looking, perhaps to explore better options.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 6
      nanos: 361813122
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
